{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-66205",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256222/assets/b8567004839dc7ac0f50c41333533e65d51d0f8682fdf63bdefbefbed82fa1e8.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Now, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nNotice that, because the cosine function is even, it is symmetric about the $y$-axis, i.e.,\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x)\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
